BLOCKING

B2 · `check studio --repo .` is blocking on W-020, and closing W-020 is the
     only thing that would clear it.

       BLOCKING (1)
         opus.red_evidence  opera/W-020.md
         missing red log(s) for behaviour(s) 9, 10, 11

     ci/reds/W-020/ holds 01-08, 12, 13, 14, 15. Behaviours 9, 10 and 11 —
     `red`'s own three — have no log in the store. The brief demands one per
     behaviour and says so twice; engineering lex §7 requires "the recorded
     red for every behaviour its spec listed" of every `done` item.

     This is round 2's F2, which round 2 recorded and declined to gate on,
     and round 2 was right to do so at the time: the rule could not see it.
     What changed is not the evidence, it is who is looking. checkRedEvidence
     (packages/cli/src/rules/evidence.ts:147) fires only for states in
     ACTIVE = {building, verifying, review}. In round 2 W-020 sat in `done`,
     outside that set, so the rule skipped it — round 2's F3. The F5 fix has
     now returned W-020 to `building`, the rule has looked at this opus for
     the first time in its life, and it blocks.

     Now follow what closing does. `runDone` (lifecycle.ts) gates on the
     front matter's probationes and nothing else; it does not consult a
     single check rule. So `bisellium done W-020` would be accepted — every
     gate on disk supports it — and the act of setting `state: done` would
     take W-020 back out of ACTIVE and the blocking finding would vanish
     from `check`. Not resolved. Hidden. The tree afterwards would read
     0 blocking and the missing reds would still not exist.

     I will not sign that. "Zero blocking required" is not a number to be
     reached by moving the opus out of the rule's line of sight; it is the
     condition the tree has to satisfy while the rule can still see it.
     Round 2 wrote F3 as "a blocking evidence rule that stops looking the
     moment an opus closes is a rule that can be outrun by closing fast."
     One round later I am holding the stopwatch. Recording a pass here would
     make round 3 the second self-serving close in this opus's history,
     after round 1 — a different mechanism, the same result.

     There is no honest path to zero blocking inside W-020, and I want that
     on the record plainly rather than dressed up:

       - Re-capturing 9-11 with `bisellium red` is impossible. The code is
         green and `red` refuses to record a passing command. That refusal is
         correct and is the best thing in this opus; I am not asking for it
         to be weakened.
       - Copying ~/.bisellium-evidence/cascade-6/w020/behaviours-09-11-red.log
         into ci/reds/W-020/09-11.log is forbidden twice over: CLAUDE.md's
         "never by hand: ci/*.log" and "evidence is produced, never
         backfilled". It is also one file covering three behaviours, so it
         would not honestly answer the rule even if it were permitted.
       - Widening ACTIVE, or exempting W-020, is W-021's file and would be
         me editing the rule that judges me.

     This is a Patron-level disposition, not a reviewer's signature. The
     decision available is to waive behaviours 9-11's reds explicitly and on
     the record — naming the interim log as what was actually produced, and
     naming the bootstrap as the reason — or to accept that W-020 does not
     close. Either is defensible. Neither is mine to take, and neither is
     `bisellium done`.


